From c06c6f7f18ac07fa9e64ac71c7be4d244de456c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:16:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assurance.attest: sign the head, because a chain does not
 detect deletion
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A hash chain is tamper-evident, not tamper-proof. Whoever holds the database
can delete the last forty records, rebuild the chain without them, and hand
over a ledger that verifies perfectly. That is a property of chains, not a
defect here, and it is true of every product that sells one.

The Cell plan already advertised "Signed head attestation for the evidence
ledger" and nothing in this codebase signed anything. That is a pricing page
making a claim the software does not back — the same failure patch 002 removed
when it deleted fabricated SBOM hashes.

assurance.attest closes both. Ed25519 throughout: small keys, small signatures,
no parameter for anybody to get wrong.

  keys          SigningKey/VerifyingKey. save() refuses to overwrite an
                existing key, and refuses to write a private key into the
                directory holding the ledger it signs — a key stored beside
                the records it protects is a decoration, because whoever can
                rewrite the records can re-sign them.
  attestation   HeadAttestation over (length, head seq, head link hash,
                basis, time, key fingerprint, predecessor). Signed payload is
                canonical JSON without the signature; each record names the
                content hash of the one before it, so the attestation log
                chains the same way the ledger does.

Three refusals carry the weight, and each is a finding rather than an error:

  * a ledger that does not verify is never attested, because a signature over
    a broken chain converts a detected break into a signed all-clear;
  * a ledger shorter than the last attestation is refused — that refusal IS
    the rewind detector;
  * a previously attested sequence that now resolves to a different link hash
    is refused. Same length, different history. verify_chain returns True for
    this; nothing else in the system catches it.

Attestations live outside the ledger, in an append-only JSONL log. Sealing an
attestation into the ledger it attests would change the head it had just
attested. The log is also the only artefact here a rewind cannot reach.

AttestationBasis records how the signer came to know the head: read_from_ledger
when it read and verified the chain itself, declared_by_holder when it was
handed three numbers. It is inside the signed bytes, so the weaker statement
cannot be read later as the stronger one — the same discipline as HashSource
and FigureBasis.

CLI: assurance attest keygen|sign|verify|log. Exit codes are the interface —
0 signed, 1 a finding, 2 could not run. A rewind must not share an exit code
with a missing file. The passphrase is read from an environment variable
because on the command line it lands in shell history and the process table.

API, and the reason the hosted shape is what it is: a service holding both the
ledger and the key satisfies nothing. So the ledger never leaves the customer's
premises. They compute their own head and send three numbers; the service
counter-signs, keeps a per-account append-only log, and applies the same three
refusals (409, not 422 — a shorter ledger is the finding, not a bad request).
The customer's administrator cannot rewind, because the removed records were
counted in a statement signed by a key nobody there holds; the service cannot
fabricate their evidence, because it never had any.

  POST /v1/ledger/attest         Cell — gates signed_attestation at last
  GET  /v1/ledger/attest/log     Cell
  POST /v1/ledger/attest/verify  free, unmetered
  GET  /v1/ledger/attest/key     free

Verification is free for the same reason the bundle re-checker is: the audience
for an attestation is a regulator, an insurer or a customer's customer, none of
whom will hold an API key here. An attestation only a paying customer can check
is worth nothing. Where the service checks signatures against its own key, the
response says that is a party checking its own work.

Every response and every verdict carries checks_skipped verbatim: that the key
provenance was not established, that records appended since the last signature
rest on the chain alone, that a counter-signature means the chain was never
seen.

pyproject gains an assurance-attest extra. cryptography is now the assurance
package's one runtime dependency, confined to this module, because writing a
signature scheme here would be the worst decision in the repository.

Tests: 43 new, 639 total, all passing. ruff clean, mypy clean on the new module.
The load-bearing ones truncate a real ledger, assert verify_chain still returns
True, and then show the attestation catching it.

